{"name":"Flood Mud","hex":"#877966","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An overflow of a large amount of water (usually disastrous) from a lake or other body of water due to excessive rainfall or other input of water.","A large number or quantity of anything appearing more rapidly than can easily be dealt with."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To overflow, as by water from excessive rainfall.","To cover or partly fill as if by a flood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flood"},{"term":"mud","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Mednyj Aleut."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixture of water and soil or fine grained sediment.","A plaster-like mixture used to texture or smooth drywall."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mud"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flood","blocks":[{"text":"A flood is an overflow of water that submerges land that is usually dry. In the sense of \"flowing water\", the word may also be applied to the inflow of the tide. Floods are of significant concern in agriculture, civil engineering and public health. Human changes to the environment often increase the intensity and frequency of flooding. Examples for human changes are land use changes such as deforestation and removal of wetlands, changes in waterway course or flood controls such as with levees. Global environmental issues also influence causes of floods, namely climate change which causes an intensification of the water cycle and sea level rise. For example, climate change makes extreme weather events more frequent and stronger. This leads to more intense floods and increased flood risk."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Urban_flood_cropped.jpg/330px-Urban_flood_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flood"},{"title":"Mud","blocks":[{"text":"Mud is loam, silt, or clay mixed with water. Mud is usually formed after rainfall or near water sources. Ancient mud deposits hardened over geological time to form sedimentary rock such as shale or mudstone. When geological deposits of mud are formed in estuaries, the resultant layers are termed bay muds. Mud has also been used for centuries as a construction resource, mostly for houses, and also as a binder. An Old English word for it was fen, now in most dialects referring to a type of wetland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/2003-11-27_Northerner_boots_in_mud.jpg/330px-2003-11-27_Northerner_boots_in_mud.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mud"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Katrina-new-orleans-flooding3-2005.jpg/500px-Katrina-new-orleans-flooding3-2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Katrina-new-orleans-flooding3-2005.jpg/1280px-Katrina-new-orleans-flooding3-2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Katrina-new-orleans-flooding3-2005.jpg/500px-Katrina-new-orleans-flooding3-2005.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"View of flooded New Orleans in the aftermath of Hurricane Katrina ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKatrina-new-orleans-flooding3-2005.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Rapid_Creek_flooding_1.jpg/500px-Rapid_Creek_flooding_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Rapid_Creek_flooding_1.jpg/1280px-Rapid_Creek_flooding_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src